rd certified  literary neuropathologists specialized  in animal models and we support a large  number of users  at our institution by doing a slight  interpretation for a wide variety of  pre-clinical studies  but we also in our lab have  the facilities and the staff  to assist people with the entire  workflow so this starts with  most in most cases submission of the  entire animals to our lab so we do  perform the necropsy tissue harvest and  fixation slide preparation so we really  do this so that we can assist people  with these um steps and also we can  perform this in a standardized manner  that is optimal for our pathology  assessment  then the slides go to our pathologist  for slight interpretation and pathology  reporting and also in a subset of  studies uh we will  perform  some form of image analysis in addition  to the pathology assessment we will  perform some form of image analysis with  software to characterize  various features within the  sections and this is typically done by  scanning the slides generating whole  site images  so being pathologists we um  the bulk of what we do is really  interpreting histopathology sections  making diagnoses so  our routine  slide that we use in most cases are hne  slide sections however  we do  incorporate in many of our studies  some form of detection of markers or  targets of interest in tissue sections  and by markers or targets i mean  either  protein that we can detect by using  antibodies for immunostore chemistry or  immunofluorescence or rna by  using rna probe with insitu  hybridization using the orange scope  assays  just  some common examples of when and how or  why we need to  detect targets within tissue sections  our most common  needs are  include  some examples that anushka already  showed so for example characterizing  immune cell infiltrates in the tumor  microenvironment that's something we do  very commonly we also do a lot of car t  cell studies and in some of those we  need to do exactly what anus anushka  showed which is to assess the target  distribution of the car t cell so we can  better  understand and assess  things like  uh of tumor on target toxicity  um so then there's really the question  when we start planning these experiments  are we going to detect the protein with  ihc or the rna with ish  and um in some cases clearly based on  the scientific question um one or the  other is more appropriate so sometimes  you know there's no choice really it's  because of  some reason specific to this study we're  only interested in the protein or only  in the rna but in many cases um  we're we consider both options equally  they're both  potentially appropriate we know that uh  in many cases if the protein is  expressed it's there we also expect to  see the rna so then we really have a  choice and what i want to talk about  today is um how do we make this choice  what are the advantages of fish over ihd  for  our people for our pathologists and our  user ihc is still for them often the  default it's still often the first thing  they ask for um i think one of the main  reasons is they're more familiar with it  we've been doing ihc in our lab for  decades  um rna-ish is newer we started doing it  with rnscope a few years ago  also with ic often we already have the  antibody or people have an antibody  they're familiar with  so um it's often the first choice but  what i want to show you today is that  clearly there are some cases where there  are very marked advantages and uh we  will want to use ish instead of ihc  um for us they're both there are both  performed in a very similar manner this  uh image shows the automated standard  that we use in our lab the leica bond rx  platform um it works both for ihc and  rna scope edge  the same technicians that were already  trained and experienced with ihc  they're able to very easily run the  issue it's  it's run in a very similar manner  and also on the same type of formula and  fixed paraffin fitted tissue sections  to illustrate this um i wanted to use  this example so this is a study uh in  which um  we use a um in which a  syngenic b16 melanoma mouse model was  used and this is uh from one of the labs  here at msk so i want to thank our user  and collaborator jill wang from the  world chat club for letting me use this  example  in this study he had two groups of mice  with b16 melanoma implanted in the flank  um the two groups where there was a  control group there was a group that was  treated with something that i'm not  going to discuss today but basically  they were comparing the two groups um  using various characteristics of these  tumors and and one of those aspects in  these studies was assessing some targets  so we did use rna probes for ish  staining for these three targets that  i'm showing here cxcl 10 il-10 and tgf  beta  the first thing that you can appreciate  and what one aspect of this that we  really like as pathologists is that we  can see not only  if the target is expressed and how how  intensely or how much of the target is  expressed but we can also see our tissue  morphology in the tissue sections  so we can see our histopathologic  features i can see that not only the  target is expressed but i can see for  example in cxcl10 that the red signal  for the rna target is within the tumor  cell within mostly the cytoplasm of the  tumor cells the pattern is very  different in il-10 i don't have signal  in the tumor cells however i do have  intense signal in a small number of  smaller cells that appear to be in  between the tumor cells these most  likely are some type of immune cell that  we could characterize exactly which type  they are with further staining  and then finally in tgf beta you can see  some very small numbers of fine dots in  the tumor cell so there is a small  amount of rna within the  tumor cells of this melanoma but the  most intense staining is within the  blood vessels  it appears to be  mostly in the endothelial cells of the  blood vessels so already i can see  qualitatively that  there's very different source of the rna  between these three markers within the  same tumor  but this is not unique to ish um this is  iht so in the same project on the same  tumors we did ihd for some immune cell  markers uh including cd3 cd8 and fox p3  for various populations of lymphocytes  and f480 for macrophages  um i also get my tissue morphology in  ihd  so  then the question is  why did i choose to do ihd in these  cases than ish for the other markers  for these immune cell marker these are  stains that are very commonly requested  in our lab so we have um  antibodies that we keep in stock we have  well optimized stains we feel that these  in most cases work well for us so that's  why  in this case we use ihc but why did we  use  why did we use the ish for the other  tree markers so these three markers were  in the case of il-10 and cxcl10 we had  never stained for them this was the  first time we had this request  in the case of tgf beta we had done in  the past imminent chemistry using a few  different antibodies  the results were never that great so we  really wanted to achieve something  better  so really having  a request for novel markers we know that  um if we need to start to find  antibodies it's not always easy to find  good antibodies and to optimize them so  and often the the  available antibodies are limited so for  some of these markers sometimes we  search  commercial vendors and sometimes there  are no antibodies available that are  already validated for immunostore  chemistry almost on formula six paraffin  embedded tissue sections for the target  and species of interest or sometimes we  do find a few antibodies but  when we start trying them we find that  they work somewhat but they're really  not that great and this brings me to the  next point is that a lot of the um  relatively poor sensitivity and  specificity that we experience with many  antibodies um this is really not an  issue with the ish that's something that  we really like about the ish staining is  that the sensitivity is very high  as well as the the sensitivity and  specificity both are really high and um  i'll show you an example later where i  have side by side um ish and ihc and and  i can show the um how how the ish is  performing much better and is more  sensitive  we also tend to save a lot of time with  ish when we are faced with novel targets  like these  um so as you probably know when we get  new antibodies for ihc even though they  often come with a recommended protocol  we really always have to optimize them  on our in our hands on our instrument to  get the best possible staining and we  have in our lab  hundreds of different antibodies uh for  which we have protocols for ihd and they  each have a slightly different protocol  so different  different epitope retrieval different  primary antibody concentration and so on  so we need to keep track of that and and  optimize each antibody accordingly  that's really not the case for the  rna-ish  for the rna scope assay we have one  protocol that's been always the same for  all the targets we've used and it's  always worked very well so typically  when we get we receive the probe we just  put it in the machine we run it with the  standard protocol and on the first day  we can start generating data um that's  quite different from the antibodies  where often we spend a few weeks to  optimize you know try a few different  antibodies optimize this pain so we save  a lot of time  and then finally um  we find that the uh ish tends to be more  suitable for quantitative assessment of  target expression and what do i mean by  that so  going back to the immune cell markers  which we assessed by  immunohistochemistry  um  in this case we're really only  interested to know if a cell is positive  or negative so we're going to use image  analysis  to quantify or to  to  classify these cells  either as simply positive or negative  and then we're going to quantify how  many positive cells  are present per area of tumor tissue  so it's simply a positive versus  negative cell for this we find that  these markers work very well  uh with ihc however  in the case of these three markers the  il-10 tgf beta and cxcl10 the scientific  question was not simply  positive versus negative it was really  about  the  degree or the amount of expression her  cell within these  samples  and for that  the  ish is very well suited because the  staining pattern is this  punctate staining so you see these  little dots and you can actually  count either  manually or you can also do this with a  software for image analysis you can  quantify how many dots are present in  each cell and it's been shown uh in some  of the validation data of these assays  that the number of dots per cell that  you see on the stain correlates with the  copy number per cell  um  so that's quite quantitative um ihc  is not going to be uh shown as dots it's  really you're going to see staining that  is either hydroplasmic or nuclear or  membrane um  you can try to  quantify the intensity or what we would  call the optical density of the  chromogen with ihc  but as many of you probably know that is  really  often not recommended because it is well  known that the optical density or the  intensity of chromogen with ihd is  really not  correlated in a quantitative manner to  the amount of targets so at best with  ihc you would get a very rough  semi-quantitative assessment of how much  protein is there and we find that the  ish is more quantitative  um  so this is what i was just describing we  do have um  these are the softwares we use uh qpat  which is open source and halo is a  commercial software both of them have a  very good  algorithm  for um ish where the dots within the  cells will be detected and then the  cells  can be or the number of dots per cell  can be quantified and you can generate  once you set this up if once you have  if you have a good stain you have good  imaging then uh you set up your image  analysis and then you can rapidly  generate in an automated manner  a large amount of quantitative data  the other um  advantage uh that i wanted to talk about  briefly for the rna-ish is the the fact  that we can really rapidly translate  from sequencing data to morphologic  analysis  so the study i showed in the study i  showed these three particular targets  were identified as  targets of interest  from a single cell rna sequencing data  so concurrently as we were doing  histology in these tumors there were  also some samples from the same study  that were going for single cell rna-seq  analysis  this generated a large amount of data  and after it was analyzed it was found  that um it appeared that  uh these three targets were of  particular interest  so then uh the uh the user asked us if  we could spain um often in my experience  the type of targets that are now  identified on single cell rna-seq um  are not the type of targets that we can  aimly find antibodies for so these are  some examples again so we're  able to rapidly go from the single cell  rna-seq to the staining of our tissue  section to validate this data and  analyze it further with the  spatial aspect  but  this rapid translation  of the sequencing data to tissue section  staining is also true in my experience  and that's something i wanted to show is  also true and is very powerful if you're  involved in um this is very different  type of study but in novel pathogen  discovery  so i want to talk briefly about  this example um  being a core facility we we mostly um  support  research done by other groups however we  do have our own small research program  being lab animal veterinarians and  veterinary pathologists were interested  in  spontaneous diseases of lab animals  including mice  and there is this disease called  inclusion body nephropathy that had been  observed for  about the past 40 years by veterinary  pathologists  so it's a spontaneous disease of mice in  which  the  kidneys undergo a chronic kidney disease  that's characterized by tubular  degeneration and necrosis as we can see  here on the right  the kidneys eventually undergo  interstitial fibrosis they start to  shrink and be misshapen  at that stage often the mice will show  evidence of clinical disease they will  become sick because of renal failure  they will have very high creatinine in  their serum  and what's very interesting is that the  tubules that are affected have these  enlarged nuclei with intranuclear  inclusion bodies shown by the arrows  which if you're familiar with  pathology of infectious diseases these  type of inclusion bodies are highly  suggestive of a virus as the cause of  the disease  we have been looking for a long time as  a potential viral cause for this disease  and and we could not find it with  traditional method  and that was until um a few years ago  with collaborators we did um viral  metagenomic analysis of  these  kidneys from these mice with chronic  renal failure so basically she did  high-triple sequencing of these kidney  tissues and went we went hunting for any  evidence of a normal viral sequence and  we did find it so we did find with our  collaborators a sequence that was  present in multiple of these kidneys the  same sequence that was compatible with a  parabol virus but it was a it was  compatible with the new or novel parvo  virus species which we named mouse  kidney parvo virus  however  we needed to show not only that this  virus sequence was present within the  tissue affected by the disease but we  needed to prove causality  and this was done by uh multiple  different experiments to show that this  virus was indeed causing the inclusion  body nephropathy but one of the key  experiments we did was with using rna  scope so very rapidly after we got the  sequence we sent it to order a custom  probe  in a couple of weeks we had received our  probe and then we applied it to the  tissue section and we were able to show  these really beautiful images that  showed how the viral rna was present  in the tissue it was present in disease  tissue it was not present in kidney  tissues of normal healthy mice and also  spatially it correlated so you can see  that the signal is present in these  abnormal tubules with enlarged nuclei  and showing attenuation of the  epithelium that's consistent with  tubular degeneration and not present in  the normal tubule so for pathologists  that's very powerful evidence if you  have colocalization of the infectious  agent with the  lesion that's really support causality  we could have done this with immunostore  chemistry but it would have taken  probably a few months to develop an  antibody and actually we eventually did  that so a few months later we did have  in addition to our ish  the  immunostochemistry assay it did show a  similar pattern of staining  but the fact that the ish allowed this  allowed us to do this so rapidly after  the sequencing data was obtained um  really helped us to you know to get this  published fairly rapidly and have very  convincing data we also found that even  though the iht worked very well in the  kidney tissue  it was not as sensitive so then  after characterizing the disease and the  kidneys we wanted to know even though  there were there was no evidence of  pathology in any other organs we wanted  to stain all other organs to see where  else this virus may replicate in the  mouth and we did find it by ish  in  a small number of cells and you see that  the intensity of staining is is much  less than the kidneys but you see these  small dots within a few cells within the  small intestine mucosa and the sequel  mucosa  and we found that this occurs actually  very within a few days after we  inoculate mice with this virus that's  the first place where we see it before  it goes to the kidneys so that's been  very useful for us to study the  pathogenesis of this infection um but  the staining is is very is much milder  in the intestine and actually when we  applied our antibody to the intestine  even though in all the mice we could see  the staining on ish we never never were  able to visualize it with antibody and  we think that probably is because the  amount of virus in the intestines is  much much lower than in the kidney and  the  we're just below the sensitivity  threshold of the immunos to chemistry  assay  this is another  similar story another novel mouse virus  that we found in our facility it's  called urine astrovirus2 it was  discovered by viral metagenomics  and  we  rapidly after obtaining the sequence we  ordered the probe we're able to confirm  that this virus was infecting our mice  we were able to study the  pathogenesis of this virus and and  follow it in different tissues after  experimental inoculation  but i also wanted to show this example  because  in this  study  we combined the rna probe for the  astrovirus rna  with antibodies for different cell types  so this is the small intestine on the  left is the  astrovirus probe in red and cd3 in green  showing that one of the main cell type  that was infected in the small intestine  were t cells with cd3 but there was also  occasional colloquialization with  epithelial cells labeled with  pancytokeratin  antibody  so um  this was my  last slide this this concludes my  presentations  presentation  again  thank you  anushka for sharing this opportunity  with me today  i also want to thank everyone for being  here and  i'll be happy to answer any questions  thank you anushka and sebastian for your  informative presentations  we will now start the live q a portion  of the webinar if you have a question  you'd like to ask please do so now just  click on the ask a question box  located on the far left of your screen  we'll answer as many of your questions  as we have time for  let's get started  our first question is for anushka  does rna scope detect insertion and  deletion of mutations  anushka  and we seem to be having some connection  issues let me ask a question for uh  sebastian  do the probes cross react for the same  target in different animal species  um  so i think this is also  possibly something anushka may want to  comment on since she's the expert on the  technology but i can give you my um  perspective as a as a user of this  technology so  uh  that's often a question we had when we  ordered probe and i think the answer is  it depends how  um  how  closely similar or different are  the same gene in different species um so  when we order the probe we've asked this  question before so for example and  that's important to us because we often  work for example with with humanized  mice where we want to detect only the  human gene and not the same gene of the  mouse so we've asked this question  before and then um they can run  the uh there is a probe design team at  acd they can run  uh an analysis within their um  with using their algorithms and they can  tell us whether or not it would cross  react in some cases they will say it  would definitely not cross-react in some  cases they say it will and the advice  they gave us when the genes if the gene  is too close between the two species  the advice to us was to use instead of  the rna scope assay was to use the base  scope ac because it can be more specific  to  shorter sequences  hey  um let's try a question for anushka  does rna scope detect insertion and  deletion of mutations  can you hear me now yes  great  yeah i just want to thank um dr monette  for his time today and for that  brilliant presentation and for this  question  um our dna is capacity which is our new  assay specifically designed to look at  genomic dna that is the assay that  would help visualize these insertions  and deletions  so not our rna scope assay but our new  dna is capacity thank you  all right  um next question this is for you anishka  does this technology work for archival  ffpe samples with poor rna quality  a great question um i think sebastian  showed a few nice examples of archival  fspe samples and a short answer is yes  absolutely  the way the probes are designed they  span across a range of  length of your target rna and they are  basically a pooled probe  of  about 20 zz pairs for rna scope and  about one to three zzz pairs so they are  a set of probes and that ensures that  even for fragmented rna you will see  some level of signal  so  um archival ffp tissues with poor iron  equality  can be used with this technology  okay thank you  our next question is for sebastian  how do you choose between chromogenic  and fluorescent assays  yeah so that's a that's a good question  um and  i think  so  as pathologists here in our lab we tend  to like the chromogenic assays because  they really give us  a really good view of our tissue  morphology so you know we can look side  by side at the h e and the chromogenic  slide  also our pathologist can examine the  chromogenic slides you know on their  regular microscope at their desk so it's  just easier to look at them routinely  however  to us the  reason to choose the fluorescence assay  is really when we're trying to do some  sort of colocalization staining um  there is a it is possible and we have  done a dual staining with chromogenic  assay it works fine if there's not  overlap between the two signal but i  would say and and i think the same is  true also i'm sure people will be  familiar with this and  many people will probably agree like if  you're using antibodies uh if there's  going to be colocalization in the same  cell within the same cell compartment  let's say cytoplasmic target within the  same cell um two chromogenic  um chrom two chromogens overlapping is  very difficult or impossible to assess  so then colocalization of multiple  marker multiplex staining um in my  experience is when you want to go with  the fluorescence  right thank you  and it looks like we have time for one  more question  uh this one i think is for anushka is  the new rna protein co-detection kit  available for automated assays  uh thanks for that question um  yes our co-detection  assay is available in both automated and  manual platforms so depending on  whichever one you prefer and whether you  have the instrument you can use either  all right well thank you again anushka  and sebastian for your time today and  your important research  we would also like to thank lab roots  and our sponsor acd for underwriting  today's educational webcast  before we go i'd like to thank the  audience for joining us today and for  their interesting questions  questions we did not have time for today  and those submitted during the on-demand  period will be addressed by the speaker  via the contact information you provided  at the time of registration  this webcast can be viewed on demand lab  routes will alert you via email when  it's available for replay  we encourage you to share that email  with your colleagues who may have missed  today's live event  until next time goodbye
 

